### BMI Percentile by Exercise Days and Gender

![Gender BMI](images/gender_boxplot.png)

---

## Interpretation

- Students who exercised more frequently tended to have slightly lower BMI percentile values.
- The observed relationship was extremely weak.
- The association was not statistically significant.
- Small gender differences were observed across BMI percentile distributions.

---

## Conclusion

No significant relationship was found between physical activity and BMI percentile.

Although students who exercised more tended to have slightly lower BMI percentile values, exercise frequency explained almost none of the variation in BMI percentile. Gender differences were observed and may influence this relationship.

